  "image_name": "21f334c94c8fc299a59bec644c7aeb7b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure uses interlocking puzzle pieces to compare natural photosynthesis with engineered solar-use technologies. On the left, natural photosynthesis is broken into four core functions: light harvesting with charge separation and electron transfer, catalytic water oxidation, separated proton/electron energy storage, and catalytic CO2 reduction. The stacked puzzle pieces suggest these are distinct but connected modules that can be combined in different ways.\n\nIn the center, several smaller puzzle-piece groupings represent selective integration of these functions rather than copying the whole biological system. Colored arrows show that different subsets of the natural processes feed into different technological designs.\n\nOn the right, these integrated combinations are mapped to major solar energy conversion routes: solar to power, solar to chemical, solar to electrochemical storage, solar to electrochemical conversion, and solar to thermal. Each route is then linked to a typical device class, such as photovoltaic cells, photocatalytic cells, photorechargeable batteries, photoelectrochemical cells, and solar thermal tandem devices. The listed materials indicate which components are emphasized in each case, including semiconductors, catalysts, redox materials, and solar absorbents.\n\nOverall, the diagram conveys that solar technologies borrow and recombine key functions of photosynthesis in modular ways to achieve different energy outputs.",
